/// Ingest data into Snowflake using Snowpipe Streaming, writing through a Snowflake pipe.
///
/// Ingest data into Snowflake using Snowpipe Streaming, writing through a Snowflake pipe
/// rather than directly into a table.
///
/// Unlike `snowflake_streaming`, which opens channels on a table, this output opens them
/// on a pipe, so any transformation defined in the pipe's `COPY INTO` (stream-static join
/// enrichment, `WHERE` filtering) runs before rows land. If you don't need those,
/// `snowflake_streaming` is the simpler choice: it creates its table for you and needs no
/// pipe.
///
/// ifndef::env-cloud[]
/// This is an Enterprise feature and requires a valid Redpanda Enterprise Edition license
/// that includes Connect. See [Redpanda Connect
/// licensing](https://docs.redpanda.com/redpanda-connect/get-started/licensing).
/// endif::[]
///
/// The `pipe` (and its table) must already exist; this output never issues `CREATE PIPE`
/// or `CREATE TABLE`. A missing pipe is reported when the output connects, before any
/// message is written. Fields with no matching column are dropped by Snowflake unless the
/// table has `ENABLE_SCHEMA_EVOLUTION = TRUE` and the pipe uses `MATCH_BY_COLUMN_NAME`,
/// in which case new fields become columns.
///
/// Moving an existing `snowflake_streaming` pipeline across is not a rename: several
/// fields are removed and `commit_timeout` changes meaning. See the [migration
/// guide](https://docs.redpanda.com/redpanda-connect/guides/migrating-to-snowflake-streaming-pipe).
///
/// ## Data types
///
/// Each message is sent to Snowflake as one JSON row and Snowflake maps its fields to
/// columns server-side; this output performs no type conversion of its own. What each
/// column type accepts is therefore Snowflake's rule, documented with the
/// [high-performance architecture's
/// limitations](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/snowpipe-streaming/snowpipe-streaming-high-performance-limitations).
/// Verified against a live account:
///
/// | Snowflake column type | JSON value |
/// | --- | --- |
/// | NUMBER, FLOAT | number, or a string holding one |
/// | BOOLEAN | boolean |
/// | CHAR, VARCHAR | string |
/// | TIMESTAMP_NTZ(9), TIMESTAMP_LTZ, DATE, TIME | RFC 3339 string; nanosecond precision is kept |
/// | VARIANT, OBJECT, ARRAY, MAP | any JSON value, including nested objects and arrays |
///
///
/// A JSON `null` into a VARIANT column lands as a VARIANT null, not SQL NULL. GEOGRAPHY
/// and GEOMETRY are not supported by Snowpipe Streaming.
///
/// Authentication can be configured using a [RSA Key
/// Pair](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/key-pair-auth).
///
/// ## Iceberg tables
///
/// Snowflake-managed Iceberg tables are supported as a target: Snowflake writes the
/// Iceberg files server-side, so nothing in this output is table-type-specific, and
/// partitioned tables work through a pipe whose `COPY INTO` reads `FROM
/// TABLE(DATA_SOURCE(TYPE => 'STREAMING'))`. Externally managed Iceberg catalogs are not
/// supported by Snowpipe Streaming. Caveats seen on live tables:
///
/// - `VARCHAR(n)` is not allowed on Iceberg tables; use `VARCHAR`.
/// - VARIANT columns need an Iceberg v3 table (`ICEBERG_VERSION = 3`).
/// - With server-side schema evolution, a new field holding a timestamp-like string can
///   put the channel into
///   `ERR_CHANNEL_SCHEMA_EVOLUTION_ICEBERG_TIME_TYPE_SCALE_NOT_SUPPORTED`. This output
///   reports that as a permanent error naming the fix; pre-create timestamp columns with
///   the type you want.
/// - Each committed batch writes one file per table partition it touched, so a low-volume
///   pipeline into a partitioned table produces many small files. Larger batches (see
///   <<request-size-and-throughput,Request size and throughput>>) and a `channel_name`
///   per source partition help.
///
/// Messages with an empty or whitespace-only body (Kafka/Redpanda tombstones, typically)
/// are not sent: a Snowflake row has no representation for them. They are acknowledged
/// upstream, counted in the `snowflake_empty_rows_skipped` metric and logged at debug
/// level. If `offset_token` is set it is still evaluated for them, so use a
/// metadata-based expression such as `${! @kafka_offset }`, not one derived from the
/// body. To land tombstones as rows, map them with a processor first.
///
/// [[choosing-a-channel-name]]
/// ## Choosing a `channel_name`
///
/// By default every message shares one constant channel, `<database>.<schema>.<pipe>`.
/// Set `channel_name` to split that up, most commonly one channel per Kafka/Redpanda
/// partition.
///
/// *The requirement:* a channel must be written by one ordered source with at most one
/// batch in flight. `offset_token` dedup compares each token against the channel's single
/// last-committed token and cannot tell "already committed by me" from "an unrelated
/// writer got ahead of me", so two token sequences sharing a channel lose rows silently.
///
/// *For a partitioned Kafka/Redpanda input*, scope the channel per partition:
///
/// ----
/// channel_name: "<pipe>-p${! @kafka_partition }"
/// ----
///
/// This is safe at any `max_in_flight` only if the input dispatches one batch per
/// partition at a time. The `redpanda` input does (unless `unordered.enabled` is set);
/// `kafka_franz` and `kafka` do not by default (`checkpoint_limit` is 1024), so set
/// `checkpoint_limit: 1` there or `max_in_flight: 1` here. If the input consumes several
/// topics, include `${! @kafka_topic }` in the name too. Never derive the name from the
/// host: after a rebalance the new owner would start a channel with no committed history
/// and re-send everything.
///
/// *For a single ordered stream* (Postgres logical replication, a single-partition
/// topic): the default channel with `max_in_flight: 1` is the simple choice. It is *not*
/// safe with `offset_token` for a partitioned source, even at `max_in_flight: 1`: each
/// partition is its own token sequence, so whichever is numerically ahead makes the
/// others' rows look like duplicates. The linter rejects the common form of this
/// (`@kafka_offset` with no `@kafka_partition` in the channel name); for anything else,
/// scope per partition or leave `offset_token` unset.
///
/// *If the requirement is violated anyway*, a batch whose tokens start below the lowest
/// token this process has already handled on the channel fails with an error naming the
/// channel and tokens, and `snowflake_out_of_order_submissions` is incremented; the error
/// recurs on every retry until `channel_name` or `max_in_flight` is fixed. A redelivery
/// from a source that keeps one batch in flight per channel never trips it: such a source
/// never goes back below where it started, however its batches are cut. The detection is
/// one-sided and process-local: it catches a second source whose tokens lie below this
/// process's, but not one whose tokens lie above or between them, nor other replicas, nor
/// anything after a restart; in those cases the rows would be dropped as duplicates with
/// no error. Recover them from your dead-letter queue or the source; redelivery alone
/// cannot re-insert them.
///
/// > **Note:** There is a limit of 10,000 streams per table -- if using more than 10k
/// > streams please reach out to Snowflake support.
///
///
/// ## Performance
///
/// This output benefits from sending multiple messages in flight in parallel for improved
/// performance. You can tune the max number of in flight messages (or message batches)
/// with the field `max_in_flight`.
///
/// This output benefits from sending messages as a batch for improved performance.
/// Batches can be formed at both the input and output level. You can find out more [in
/// this doc](https://docs.redpanda.com/redpanda-connect/configuration/batching).
///
/// [[request-size-and-throughput]]
/// ## Request size and throughput
///
/// Each batch is one request per channel. Request bodies are zstd-compressed by default
/// (`compression`), and Snowflake's 4 MB per-request limit applies to the body as sent,
/// after compression. A batch whose body is still over the limit is sent as several
/// requests, split by message, each with its own offset-token range, so a large batch
/// never fails for size; only a single message over the limit on its own cannot be sent
/// (see `tolerate_row_errors`). Typical JSON compresses 5–8×, so 16–20 MB of
/// messages usually fits in one request, and anything under about 3.5 MB always does.
/// Memory per in-flight batch is roughly twice the uncompressed messages (the batch and
/// the encoded body) plus the compressed body. Watch
/// `snowflake_compressed_output_size_bytes` against the message bytes to see your ratio.
/// Without `offset_token`, a batch sent as several requests can be duplicated if a later
/// request fails after an earlier one committed: the whole batch is redelivered and the
/// committed requests are sent again, so for large at-least-once batches prefer sizes
/// that fit one request.
///
/// *With the `redpanda` input, size batches at the input*, through
/// `max_yield_batch_bytes` (default `32KB`, far below what one request carries), rather
/// than with an output `batching` policy: an output policy pools every partition's
/// messages into one request, which serialises the partitions.
///
/// *A channel carries one batch at a time.* The next batch for a channel waits until the
/// previous one has committed, so per-channel throughput is bounded by request size over
/// commit latency (typically a few seconds in-region). Spread load across channels with a
/// per-partition `channel_name`. Pipelining appends within a channel is planned.
///
/// Status: beta
